The number $n_p$ of Sylow $p$-subgroups satisfies $n_p \equiv 1 \pmod{p}$ and $n_p \mid (p+1)$. Since $G$ has no normal Sylow $p$-subgroup, $n_p > 1$. As $p+1 < 2p$ (for $p \geq 3$), the only values in $\{1,\dots,p+1\}$ congruent to $1 \pmod{p}$ are $1$ and $p+1$. Thus:
$$n_p = p+1.$$
Consequently $|N_G(P)| = |G|/n_p = p$ for each Sylow $p$-subgroup $P$, so $N_G(P) = P$.

